{
  "description": "MAINTAINER RULING #984 at the EIGHTH main-line container found in this slice, and the second that executes no statements. An `each` iteration narrows the addressed set to one turtle and runs a body, so it is a unit of main-line progress exactly as a loop iteration is -- but the boundary that serves every other container fires per STATEMENT, so an empty `each` body had none. Measured before this was pinned: two turtles with `each [ ]` gave a queued `every` occurrence FOUR handler firings where both `each [ print 0 ]` and an equivalent `repeat 2 [ ]` gave FIVE, so the ruling's guarantee depended on whether the learner's per-turtle body happened to contain something. The iteration is now charged against the execution budget and carries the same empty-body boundary, and all three forms agree at five. The empty body is the whole point of the fixture and must not be `improved` into a non-empty one. Requires two turtles so the iteration count is observable: `each` over a single turtle cannot distinguish a per-iteration boundary from a per-statement one.",
